1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:05,000
This is Task 10, continuous testing in Jenkins.

2
00:00:05,000 --> 00:00:06,000
The idea is simple.

3
00:00:06,000 --> 00:00:09,000
Each test stage runs a command.

4
00:00:09,000 --> 00:00:18,000
When pytest finds a failing test, it exits with a non zero code, Jenkins marks the stage failed, and every later stage is skipped.

5
00:00:18,000 --> 00:00:30,000
These are the lines of the Jenkinsfile that do it: the two test commands, the report publishing that always runs, and the clean up line at the start.

6
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:35,000
Notice that package, images, publish and deploy come after the tests.

7
00:00:39,000 --> 00:00:47,000
To prove the quality gate, I introduced a deliberate defect on a throw away branch called experiment slash defect demo.

8
00:00:47,000 --> 00:00:52,000
It has two commits: first the defect, then the corrective fix.

9
00:00:52,000 --> 00:00:58,000
And this second command proves the safety: it lists the branches that contain the defect commit.

10
00:00:58,000 --> 00:01:01,000
Only the two experiment branches.

11
00:01:01,000 --> 00:01:04,000
The defect was never merged into develop or main.

12
00:01:08,000 --> 00:01:09,000
This is the defect.

13
00:01:09,000 --> 00:01:18,000
In the overlap rule of the booking data access layer, the strict comparison, check in less than check out, became less than or equal.

14
00:01:18,000 --> 00:01:27,000
A tiny off by one change, but it wrongly says that a room is booked when one guest checks out on the same day another checks in.

15
00:01:27,000 --> 00:01:32,000
A subtle bug like this is exactly what automated tests exist to catch.

16
00:01:36,000 --> 00:01:39,000
Now I reproduce what Jenkins saw, safely.

17
00:01:39,000 --> 00:01:48,000
I clone the repository into a disposable folder, check out the defect commit, and run the same tests the pipeline runs.

18
00:01:48,000 --> 00:01:53,000
A test fails, the summary says failed, and the exit code is one.

19
00:01:53,000 --> 00:01:57,000
That non zero code is exactly what makes Jenkins stop.

20
00:01:57,000 --> 00:02:00,000
Nothing in my real project changed.

21
00:02:09,000 --> 00:02:11,000
Now the corrective commit.

22
00:02:11,000 --> 00:02:17,000
I check out the fix in the same disposable clone and run the same tests.

23
00:02:17,000 --> 00:02:20,000
They all pass, and the exit code is zero.

24
00:02:20,000 --> 00:02:26,000
In Jenkins, this is build number three: green, and the deployment stage runs.

25
00:02:26,000 --> 00:02:30,000
The fix restored the strict comparison, one line in one file.

26
00:02:39,000 --> 00:02:48,000
I delete the disposable clone and confirm that my project is untouched: same branch, and no new changes except this demonstration folder.

27
00:02:51,000 --> 00:02:54,000
Studying build two, I found a real problem.

28
00:02:54,000 --> 00:03:01,000
Its test report listed five Selenium tests as passed, although the Selenium stage had been skipped.

29
00:03:01,000 --> 00:03:09,000
Jenkins reuses its workspace, so the old report from build one was still there and was published again.

30
00:03:09,000 --> 00:03:18,000
The fix, on branch fix slash jenkins stale reports, is this: the checkout stage now deletes the reports and dist folders first.

31
00:03:18,000 --> 00:03:23,000
A build can no longer publish a report from an older build.

32
00:03:28,000 --> 00:03:30,000
One last detail you may be asked about.

33
00:03:30,000 --> 00:03:39,000
The Jenkins builds were made before the history was rewritten, so Jenkins and the evidence logs show the original commit IDs.

34
00:03:39,000 --> 00:03:51,000
This map links each original ID to the current one: the defect commit, the corrective fix, and the stale report fix are all listed, with the same subject lines.

35
00:03:51,000 --> 00:03:52,000
Same changes, new hash.

36
00:03:58,866 --> 00:04:03,866
This is the job's stage view, and the real defect experiment.

37
00:04:03,866 --> 00:04:04,866
The rows are builds.

38
00:04:04,866 --> 00:04:12,866
Build number one is green: the baseline, version one point zero point zero, commit six four three five one f e.

39
00:04:12,866 --> 00:04:22,866
Build number two, the one with the deliberate defect, is red, and it fails at exactly one place: the unit and integration tests stage.

40
00:04:22,866 --> 00:04:25,866
Build number three is green again.

41
00:04:26,866 --> 00:04:27,866
Build two's console.

42
00:04:27,866 --> 00:04:31,866
Pytest reports one failed, eighty one passed.

43
00:04:31,866 --> 00:04:38,866
The failure has a name: test overlap rules, with the dates the eighth to the tenth of January 2030.

44
00:04:38,866 --> 00:04:45,866
The assertion says room one oh one was expected to be free, but it was reported booked.

45
00:04:45,866 --> 00:04:48,866
That is exactly the off by one defect.

46
00:04:49,866 --> 00:04:51,866
And here is the quality gate.

47
00:04:51,866 --> 00:04:59,866
Right after the failure, for every following stage Jenkins writes: stage skipped due to earlier failures.

48
00:04:59,866 --> 00:05:04,866
E2E tests, package source, build images, publish images, and deploy.

49
00:05:04,866 --> 00:05:09,866
Nothing was packaged, nothing was published, and nothing was deployed.

50
00:05:09,866 --> 00:05:15,866
The pipeline ends with its own message: pipeline failed, nothing was deployed.

51
00:05:16,866 --> 00:05:22,866
The test result page of build two lists the failing test by name, with its error.

52
00:05:22,866 --> 00:05:34,866
This page also contains something surprising, which I will explain in a moment: a package called tests dot e2e with five passed tests, although the Selenium stage never ran in this build.

53
00:05:35,866 --> 00:05:37,866
This is the stale report bug.

54
00:05:37,866 --> 00:05:44,866
Build two never packaged anything, yet its artifacts list a source archive and a deployment record.

55
00:05:44,866 --> 00:05:45,866
They are not new.

56
00:05:45,866 --> 00:05:54,866
They are left over from build number one, because Jenkins reuses its workspace, and the archive step collected the old files.

57
00:05:54,866 --> 00:06:01,866
That is why build two showed five passing Selenium tests: they came from build one's report.

58
00:06:02,866 --> 00:06:03,866
What happened to staging?

59
00:06:03,866 --> 00:06:12,866
The deployment record of build one says staging ran version one point zero point zero, commit six four three five one f e.

60
00:06:12,866 --> 00:06:17,866
Because build two's deploy stage was skipped, the staging containers were never touched.

61
00:06:17,866 --> 00:06:22,866
So that version stayed active the whole time the defect build was failing.

62
00:06:23,866 --> 00:06:28,866
After the corrective commit, build three ran the same pipeline.

63
00:06:28,866 --> 00:06:29,866
All stages are green.

64
00:06:29,866 --> 00:06:40,866
Its deployment record shows the staging environment now runs version one point zero point zero, commit three zero c six a zero five, the corrected code.

65
00:06:40,866 --> 00:06:45,866
So the pipeline blocked the defective version, and released the fixed one.

66
00:06:46,866 --> 00:06:53,866
After I fixed the stale report problem, I repeated the experiment as build number nine.

67
00:06:53,866 --> 00:06:55,866
It fails on the same test.

68
00:06:55,866 --> 00:07:00,866
But look at its test result: only the tests that really ran.

69
00:07:00,866 --> 00:07:03,866
There is no tests dot e2e package any more.

70
00:07:03,866 --> 00:07:08,866
The report does not claim Selenium tests that did not happen.

71
00:07:08,866 --> 00:07:11,866
Eighty one passed, one failed, total eighty two.

72
00:07:12,866 --> 00:07:19,866
And the artifacts of build nine: only the one test report that this build produced.

73
00:07:19,866 --> 00:07:24,866
No source archive, no deployment record, no health file from earlier builds.

74
00:07:24,866 --> 00:07:27,866
The same bug can no longer happen.

75
00:07:28,866 --> 00:07:36,866
This is the fix, in every build log, here build eleven: the very first command after checkout is rm minus rf reports dist.

76
00:07:36,866 --> 00:07:44,866
Old outputs are deleted before anything runs, so a build can never reuse an old report as if it were new.

77
00:07:44,866 --> 00:07:55,866
That closes the experiment: the defect was caught by the tests, deployment was blocked, the fix was released, and a bug in the pipeline itself was found and fixed.
